{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab and this experience shaped his diplomatic approach toward Muslim neighbors. He often sought peace treaties rather than constant warfare because he understood the fragile nature of the Latin kingdoms in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond became count while still a young man following the assassination of his father Raymond II. The Knights Hospitaller held him captive for several years after the defeat at Inab where many Crusader leaders died or were taken prisoner. This period of imprisonment allowed him to learn about Islamic culture and politics directly from his captors. His release came through a ransom payment and this event marked the beginning of his long tenure as a ruler who valued negotiation over blind aggression. He married Hodierna of Jerusalem to strengthen ties with the royal family and secure his position within the complex feudal hierarchy of Outremer.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nKing Baldwin IV suffered from leprosy so he appointed Raymond as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem to govern on his behalf. This role placed him at the center of intense political friction between the court party led by Guy of Lusignan and the noble faction supporting the leper king. Raymond opposed the marriage between Sibylla and Guy because he feared their rule would bring disaster to the realm. His refusal to acknowledge Guy as king after Baldwin IV died sparked a internal crisis that weakened the Christian defenses just before the rise of Saladin. The barons initially supported Raymond as a potential alternative monarch yet he ultimately stepped aside to prevent civil war.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nThe disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 ended in a crushing defeat for the Crusader forces and Raymond was one of the few commanders who escaped capture. He had warned against marching across the arid plateau without water but his advice was ignored by Guy of Lusignan. The loss led to the fall of Jerusalem and the collapse of most Crusader holdings in the region. Raymond retreated to Tripoli where he died later that same year possibly from illness or despair over the catastrophe he had tried to prevent. Historians view him as a pragmatic leader whose warnings went unheeded due to factional strife within the kingdom. His life illustrates the challenges of maintaining a Christian presence in a hostile environment while navigating treacherous internal politics. The failure to listen to his counsel cost the Crusaders dearly and changed the course of medieval history in the Middle East forever.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004940061}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0023330823}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.18271562}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -4.5538985e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.5299635e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.3841887e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0016073382}, {"token": " played", "log